Q: Set env variables before running/debugging a single C# integration test in VS Code A: <p>I imagine your method under test to look like, more or less</p>
<pre><code>public class SystemUnderTest
{
    public void MethodToBeTested()
    {
        // Somewhere down below you access env variable
        var envVar = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(&quot;MY_VAR_NAME&quot;);
        // use envVar
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And now you want to unit test <code>MethodToBeTested</code>, but can't mock environment variable.</p>
<p>Since <code>IConfiguration</code> is used to also store environment variables, you can use it in your method as well :)</p>
<p>This could be refactored in two ways:</p>
<ul>
<li>following dependency injection, accept <code>IConfiguration</code> as constructor parameter</li>
<li>accept <code>IConfiguration</code> as parameter of the method</li>
</ul>
<p>After that you can easily mock <code>IConfiguration</code> interface to your liking, as usual :)</p>
<p>Here's how you could refactor:</p>
<p><strong>1st option</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class SystemUnderTest
{
    public void MethodToBeTested(IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        // Somewhere down below you access env variable
        var envVar = configuration[&quot;MY_VAR_NAME&quot;];
        // use envVar
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2nd option</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class SystemUnderTest
{
    private readonly IConfiguration _configuration;

    public SystemUnderTest(IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _configuration = configuration
    }

    public void MethodToBeTested()
    {
        // Somewhere down below you access env variable
        var envVar = _configuration[&quot;MY_VAR_NAME&quot;];
        // use envVar
        // ...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Such tests are less flaky, as they don't depend on how they are run, but rather how they are written.</p>
<p>On top of that, you also have entire test setup in the test itself in arrange section. This provides much better readability, IMO.</p>
